        "title": "Search parking",
        "description": "Search for available parking spaces near a location. Returns up to 10 spaces, each with an id, name, distance, price, and rating. A missing location is the only reason to ask the user a question before calling this tool — a missing date or time is never a reason to ask; compute arrival/departure yourself per the priority rules on those fields and call this tool directly. Be sure which place the user means before searching: if this tool responds with AMBIGUOUS_LOCATION, ask the user to choose from the options it lists, then call again with place_id set to their choice.",
        "inputSchema": {
          "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
          "type": "object",
          "properties": {
            "location": {
              "type": "string",
              "description": "Address, postcode, or landmark to search near. Pass the user's own wording — do not translate or guess a location they haven't given."
            },
            "place_id": {
              "description": "Only ever a place_id returned by a previous search_parking disambiguation response in this conversation. Never invent one, and never source one from anywhere else.",
              "type": "string"
            },
            "arrival": {
              "type": "string",
              "pattern": "^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2} \\d{2}:\\d{2}$",
              "description": "Arrival datetime in format \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm\". Never ask the user to clarify — apply the first matching rule: (1) explicit date+time given, use them; (2) \"this weekend\"/\"next weekend\", use 08:00 on the upcoming Saturday; (3) a date given but no time, use 08:00 on that date; (4) a time given but no date, use today if that time hasn't passed yet, otherwise tomorrow; (5) nothing given at all, use now + 15 minutes."
            },
            "departure": {
              "type": "string",
              "pattern": "^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2} \\d{2}:\\d{2}$",
              "description": "Departure datetime in format \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:mm\". Never ask the user to clarify — apply the first matching rule: (1) explicit date+time given, use them; (2) \"this weekend\"/\"next weekend\", use 23:00 on the upcoming Sunday (spans the whole weekend); (3) a date given but no time, use 18:00 on that date; (4) a time given but no date, use arrival + 4 hours unless a duration/end time was given; (5) nothing given at all, use arrival + 4 hours."
            }
          },
